[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Distributed Systems and Network Topologies in Performance Testing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Distributed systems and network topologies form the backbone of modern enterprise applications. For performance engineers, understanding how these components interact is crucial for designing accurate load tests, identifying bottlenecks, and predicting system behavior under stress."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of Distributed Systems"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A distributed system consists of multiple autonomous computers that communicate through a computer network and coordinate their actions by passing messages. When performance testing these environments, engineers must account for concurrency, partial failures, and latency."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Latency and Network Delays: Data traveling across physical nodes introduces latency, impacting response times."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Consistency: CAP theorem considerations (Consistency, Availability, Partition tolerance) dictate how systems react during network splits."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Load Balancing: Distributing incoming network traffic across multiple backend servers to ensure high availability and reliability."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Network Topologies"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The physical or logical arrangement of nodes in a network significantly affects throughput and failure domains. Performance tests must simulate the specific topology of the production environment to yield meaningful results."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Star Topology: All nodes are connected to a central hub. A failure in the hub isolates all attached nodes."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mesh Topology: Devices are interconnected with multiple pathways between nodes, offering high redundancy and fault tolerance."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bus Topology: Nodes are connected via a single common communication line, which can become a major bottleneck under heavy load."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Incorporating Topologies into Performance Test Scripts"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using tools like JMeter or k6, you should configure your load generators to mimic the geographic distribution and network hops of your production topology. Below is an example of configuring distributed execution in k6 using multiple load generators."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 100 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 500 },
    { duration: '2m', target: 0 },
  ],
};

export default function() {
  const res = http.get('https://api.example.com/v1/distributed-resource');
  check(res, {
    'status was 200': (r) => r.status === 200,
    'transaction time < 200ms': (r) => r.timings.duration < 200,
  });
  sleep(1);
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Distributed Performance Testing"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simulate Realistic Network Conditions: Use traffic shaping tools to introduce packet loss, jitter, and bandwidth limits matching your network topology."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor All Hops: Ensure Application Performance Monitoring (APM) tools trace requests across every microservice and network boundary."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Test for Cascading Failures: Introduce latency into a single node to observe how the broader distributed system degrades."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding the intersection of distributed systems and network topologies, performance engineers can build resilient architectures and design rigorous testing strategies that reflect real-world constraints."}]
